2. Create or Clone a Repository
--------------------------------

    Command                          | Description
    ---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------
    git init                         | Create a new empty repository in the current folder
    git clone <repo_url>             | Copy (clone) an existing remote repository


    Example 1 — git init
    --------------------
        mkdir myproject                   # Create a folder
        cd myproject                      # Enter the folder
        git init                          # Initialize an empty local repo

        Result:
        myproject/                        | Working tree (currently empty)
        └── .git/                         | Local repository data (history, commits)

        Note:
        - After `git init`, the working tree has no files yet.
        - You must create files, then `git add` them before committing.


    Example 2 — git clone
    ---------------------
        git clone https://github.com/user/project.git

        Result:
        project/                          | Folder created
        ├── (Working tree)                | Editable files from the remote repo
        └── .git/                         | Local repository data (history, commits)



3.	Check Status and History
----------------------------

	1.	git status
		# Shows:
		# - Untracked files (not yet staged)
		# - Staged files (ready to commit)
		# - Modified but unstaged files
		# Helps you know what’s going on before committing.

	2.	git log
		# Shows the complete commit history with:
		# - Commit IDs
		# - Authors
		# - Dates
		# - Commit messages

	3.	git log --oneline --graph --all
		# Shows a compact view of history:
		# - One line per commit
		# - ASCII branch/merge graph
		# - All branches

	4.	git show <commit_id>
		# Shows details for a specific commit:
		# - Changes made
		# - Author/date
		# - Commit message

	5.	git diff
		# Shows differences between working tree and staging area (unstaged changes).
		# → “What did I change but haven’t staged?”

	6.	git diff --staged
		# Shows differences between staging area and the last commit (staged changes).
		# → “What will be committed if I commit now?”


	Example: `git diff` vs `git diff --staged`
	------------------------------------------
		1️⃣ Edit `audioPlayer.c`
			- Added: `//changes` at the top.
			- `git diff`
				→ Shows the change (unstaged).
			- `git diff --staged`
				→ Shows nothing (file not staged yet).

		2️⃣ Stage the file
			- `git add audioPlayer.c`
			- `git diff --staged`
				→ Shows the change (ready to commit).
			- `git diff`
				→ Shows nothing (working tree matches staging area).

		Summary:
			- **Before `git add`** → Change is *only* in working tree → `git diff` shows it.
			- **After `git add`**  → Change is in staging area → `git diff --staged` shows it.


		Removing Changes
		----------------
			•	If staged:
					git restore --staged audioPlayer.c    # Unstage changes
					git restore audioPlayer.c            # Discard from working tree
			•	If not staged:
					git restore audioPlayer.c            # Discard changes directly


4.	Stage and Commit Changes
----------------------------

	1.	git add file.txt
		# Stages a specific file for commit.

	2.	git add .
		# Stages all changes (new, modified, deleted files) in the current directory.

	3.	git commit -m "Meaningful message"
		# Commits all staged changes with a descriptive commit message.
		# Only commits what’s already staged.
		# If you made changes but didn’t run git add, those changes are ignored in this commit.
		# Safer, because you control exactly what gets committed.	
		# git add file1.txt
		# git commit -m "Update file1"
		# → Commits only file1.txt (even if other files were changed).

		
	4.	git commit -am "Message"
		# Automatically stages and commits all modified, already-tracked files.
		# Does not stage untracked files (new files) — you still need git add for those.
		# git commit -am "Quick fix"
		# → Commits all modified tracked files in one step.

	5.	git commit --amend
		# Modifies the most recent commit.
		# Uses:
		#   - Change the last commit message
		#   - Add forgotten changes to the last commit
		#
		# Example:
		#   1️⃣ Make a commit → `git commit -m "Initial commit"`
		#   2️⃣ Realize you forgot a file → `git add missing_file`
		#   3️⃣ Run → `git commit --amend`
		#       → Adds the new file into the last commit (instead of creating a new commit)
		#
		# Note:
		#   - This rewrites history for the last commit
		#   - Avoid using it if the commit is already pushed to a shared remote


5. Branching
-------------
	
	git branch                                       # List branches in the repository. Current branch is marked with an asterisk (*).
	git branch <branch_name>                         # Creates a new branch (but does not switch to it).
	git checkout <branch_name>                       # Switch to another branch
	git checkout -b <branch_name>                    # Create & switch to new branch
