	git branch -D <branch_name>                      # Force delete a branch. Deletes even if there are unmerged changes.




6. Merging & Rebasing
----------------------

   1. git merge <branch_name>
        # Merges another branch into the current branch.
        # Creates a merge commit if branches have diverged.

   2. git rebase <branch_name>
        # Moves (reapplies) your current branch commits on top of another branch.
        # Keeps history linear, avoids merge commits.

   3. git rebase -i HEAD~n
        #-i means interactive.
        # Interactive rebase for the last n commits.
        # HEAD~n means "the last n commits from the current branch"
        # This command lets you open an editor where you can see the last n commits and choose to:
        # Allows you to edit, squash commits(combine this commit into previous one), reorder commits, or drop commits.


    The Commit Graph Before Merge/Rebase
    ------------------------------------
        main branch:    a --- b --- c --- d
        feature branch:             |
                                    E --- F

        - main has commits: a, b, c, d
        - feature has commits: E, F (starting from commit c)


    git merge
    ---------
        Command:
            git checkout main
            git merge feature

        What happens:
            - Takes commits from 'feature' (E, F) and combines them into 'main'.
            - Creates a new merge commit G that ties the histories together.

        Result:
            a --- b --- c --- d -------- G
                        |               |
                        E --- F --------

        Key points:
            - Extra commit: G (“Merge branch 'feature' into main”).
            - History preserved: Both branch timelines are intact.
            - Good for: Team workflows where history clarity is important.
           -G → d → F → E → c → b → a





    git rebase
    ----------
        Command:
            git checkout feature
            git rebase main

        What happens:
            - Replays commits E and F on top of main’s d.
            - No merge commit is created; history becomes linear.

        Result:
            a --- b --- c --- d --- E' --- F'
            (E' and F' are the same changes as E and F, but with new commit IDs.)

    
    Key Differences
    ---------------
        Feature         git merge                       git rebase
        --------------------------------------------------------------------
        Extra commit    Yes (merge commit)              No
        History         Keeps both branch timelines     Rewrites into a single timeline
        Commit IDs      Unchanged                       New commit IDs
        Use case        Preserve full history clarity   Clean, linear history


7. Working with Remotes
------------------------

    git remote -v
        # Lists connected remote repositories and their URLs.
        # origin is the default name Git gives to the remote repository you cloned from or connected to
        # origin  https://github.com/user/myrepo.git (fetch)
	  origin  https://github.com/user/myrepo.git (push)

        

    git remote add origin <url>
        # Connects a local repo to a remote repository.

   git fetch origin
   	 # origin is the default name Git gives to the remote repository you cloned from or connected to
  	 # Downloads changes from remote without merging into your branch.
  	 # It connects to the remote repository called origin (usually your GitHub repo).
   	 # It downloads all the new commits, branches, and tags from the remote that you don’t have locally yet.
   	 # But it does NOT change or update your current working files or branch.
   
    # Analogy:
   	# You and your friend both have copies of the same book (code repo).
   	# Your friend writes some new pages and adds them to their book.
   	# You want to see what your friend added, but you don’t want to change your book yet.
   	# It downloads your friend’s new pages and keeps them in a separate folder inside .git.
  	# Your original book (your current branch) does NOT change.
   	# You can look at these new pages anytime, but they’re not mixed into your book yet.
  	# To update your branch with these changes, you run:
   	# git merge origin/main
   	# (or)
    	# git pull
    	# which fetches and merges the changes.


    git fetch --all
    	# Fetches updates from all configured remote repositories.
    	# If you have multiple remotes (e.g., origin, upstream),
    	# this command downloads new commits, branches, and tags from all of them,
    	# but does NOT merge or change your current branches.
    	# downloads updates from all your remotes and stores them in separate remote-tracking branches inside your local repository (.git)— just like git fetch origin does for a single remote


    git push origin <branch>
        # Pushes commits from your local branch to the remote.
        # to the remote repository named 'origin' (usually your GitHub repo).
        # origin is the default name Git gives to the remote repository you cloned from or connected to
        # This updates the remote branch with your latest changes,


   git push -u origin <branch>
   	#  Push this new branch to the remote and set it as default upstream
    	# This command does two things at once:
    	# 1. It uploads your local branch (with your new commits) to the remote called 'origin'.
    	# 2. It tells Git: "Hey, from now on, when I run 'git push' or 'git pull' without any extra words,
    	#    use this remote branch as the default for this local branch."
    	#
    	# So, next time you just run 'git push' or 'git pull' without writing 'origin <branch>',
    	# Git will know exactly where to send or get updates.
    	# Next time, you can simply run:git push


    git pull origin <branch>
        # Fetches and merges changes from remote into current branch.
        # It fetches and merges just the changes from that one branch into your current local branch.
        # You are currently on your local 'main' branch
	# git checkout main
	# Pull changes from the remote 'main' branch into your local 'main'
	# git pull origin main
	


    git pull --rebase origin <branch>
    	# Fetches changes from the remote branch <branch> on 'origin'.
    	# Instead of merging, it reapplies your local commits on top of the fetched commits.
    	# This avoids creating a merge commit and keeps the commit history linear and cleaner.


8. Cherry-Pick
---------------

	git cherry-pick <commit_id>
		# Applies a specific commit from another branch into your current branch.

	git cherry-pick <start_commit>^..<end_commit>
		# Applies a sequence of commits from <start_commit> (inclusive) up to <end_commit> (inclusive)
		# onto your current branch.
		# Useful when you want to apply multiple specific commits without merging the entire branch.

	Example:

		Commit history:
			a111111 Commit A  
			b222222 Commit B  
			c333333 Commit C  
			d444444 Commit D  

		You want to cherry-pick commits B and C onto your current branch.

		Command:
			git cherry-pick b222222^..c333333

		Explanation:
			- b222222^ means the parent of commit B, so the range starts from commit B (inclusive).
			- This will apply commits B and C onto your current branch.
			-Commit IDs uniquely identify a commit everywhere in your repository — across all branches.

		Simple example:

			# Assume you are on branch 'master'.
			# Another branch 'feature' has commits B and C you want to apply to master.

			git checkout master
			git cherry-pick b222222^..c333333




9. Stash Changes
-----------------

    git stash
        # Saves your changes temporarily and cleans working directory.
        # Cleans your working directory to match the last commit.
        # Good for switching branches without committing.

    git stash list
        # Lists all saved stashes.
        # Each stash is shown with an identifier like stash@{0}, stash@{1}, etc.

    git stash pop
        # Applies the most recent stash and removes it from stash list.

    git stash apply stash@{n}
        # Applies a specific stash without removing it from stash list.
        # Does not remove the stash from the list, so you can apply it again later if needed.



10. Undo Changes
-----------------

    git restore file.txt
    	# You edited file.txt but haven't staged it yet
        # Discards local changes in a file (unstaged changes).

    git reset --soft HEAD~1
        # Moves HEAD back one commit but keeps all changes staged for recommit.
        # scenario**
        # You made a commit but realize you want to change the commit message or add more changes before finalizing.
        # The last commit is undone.
        # All your changes are still staged (ready to commit).
        # You can edit files or change the commit message
        # Commit again with new message or after adding more changes

    git reset --mixed HEAD~1
        # Moves HEAD back one commit and unstages changes (default).

    git reset --hard HEAD~1
        # Moves HEAD back one commit and discards all changes.

    git revert <commit_id>
        # creates a new commit that undoes the changes made by that old commit.
        # It doesn’t remove or erase the old commit — it just adds a new commit that reverses its effect.
        # safe : Because it does not change history (no commits are deleted).




11. Tags
---------

    git tag v1.0
        # Creates a lightweight tag (just a label pointing to the current commit).
        # No extra information is saved.


    git tag -a v1.0 -m "Version 1.0 release"
        # Creates an annotated tag with a message and metadata.
        # git tag: Command to create a tag in Git.
        # -a v1.0: Creates an annotated tag named v1.0.
        # Annotated tags store extra info like:
        	Tagger’s name
        	Date/time
        	Tag message
		-m "Version 1.0 release": Adds a message describing the tag, like a commit message.


    git push origin v1.0
        # Pushes a specific tag to remote.
        # Push the tag v1.0 to the remote called origin

    git push origin --tags
        # Pushes all local tags to remote.
        #  sends all tags you’ve created locally (e.g., v1.0, v2.0) to the remote repository named origin, so others can access those version tags too.


12. Repo Commands (for Android / Gerrit-based projects)
--------------------------------------------------------

    repo init -u <manifest_url> -b <branch_name>
        # Initializes a repo with given manifest and branch.
        # A manifest is basically a file (usually XML) that lists a collection of multiple Git repositories along with:
            - Their locations (URLs)
            - Which branch or revision to use
            - Where each repo should be placed in your local folder structure
        # Manifest = A list that manages many repos together as one big project.

        **Why use it?
            # Instead of cloning many repos one by one, the manifest lets you manage all repos as a single project.
            # The `repo` tool uses the manifest to download and organize all repos automatically.
            # This makes working with large projects (like Android source) much easier.




    repo sync
        # Syncs all projects to latest revisions.
        # Downloads and updates all repositories listed in the manifest
        # Ensures your local copies are up-to-date with the latest changes from remote repos
        # Useful to keep the entire multi-repo project in sync


    repo sync -j<N>
        # Syncs with N parallel jobs (faster).
        # Syncs all projects like `repo sync`
        # Uses N parallel jobs to download/update repos concurrently
        # Speeds up syncing by doing multiple operations at once


    repo status
        # Shows working branch for all projects.
        # Shows the current working branch and status for all projects in the repo workspace
        # Helps you see which branch each project is on and if there are local changes


    repo branches
        # Lists all available branches across all projects in the repo workspace
        # Helps you see what branches exist in each project managed by repo


    repo start <branch_name> <project>
        # Creates and switches to a new branch named <branch_name> in the specified <project>
        # Used to begin working on a feature or fix in that particular project


    repo upload
        # Uploads committed changes to Gerrit for code review.
        # Sends your committed changes to Gerrit for code review
        # Allows team members to review, comment, and approve your code before merging


    repo forall -c 'git status'
        # Runs `git status` in all projects.
        # Executes the command `git status` inside every project/repository
        # Useful to quickly check the status of all repos in your repo workspace



